#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9455e98a-4713-54c6-af68-bccd092a2ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{y + 1}{2} = \\frac{z - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123045} {:answer/id #uuid "21291d24-effb-516e-945a-51a6535c2133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = y + 1 = z - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123046} {:answer/id #uuid "5095f082-079e-5ce9-b5ca-ce5407b52ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{y + 1}{2} = z - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123047} {:answer/id #uuid "6f85db0b-f965-5a7a-8ae9-897fe1b293ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2} = \\frac{y + 1}{2} = z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123048} {:answer/id #uuid "768be4cc-7f24-540b-b0f8-718016dd08a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-y - 1 = \\frac{z - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123049}] :answer-field/id #uuid "322e8e14-4251-59c0-abc0-0a9f1a6ccd3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123044} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccafabf2-27cf-5b44-8930-a54e452bc954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-y-1=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385385} {:answer/id #uuid "67a0969d-2efa-50ec-a3a4-619fb8bb16fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385386} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6a97a3-5af6-531a-870a-ae08853e320f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385387} {:answer/id #uuid "7632f3d8-1e29-5bdf-93fe-9967dfc64633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385388} {:answer/id #uuid "247f5172-7db7-511c-8fd9-7db9f6287753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67a0969d-2efa-50ec-a3a4-619fb8bb16fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6b56a1-3889-545f-a00d-30961c2c2ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78adb807-d26f-59bb-a8f7-9d0e5cc88b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-37515" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,-1,1) & =(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\text{ }i+[(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+2(-1)]\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i-2\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{-1}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbad3809-ba80-59f9-ba4c-d639a5fee9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i - 2j + 4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123069} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfccc86-e9f7-5243-8a84-7790a342f294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4j + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123070} {:answer/id #uuid "47a26aab-bf98-583d-a150-8a48058ef8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4i - 2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123071} {:answer/id #uuid "765db6cc-9dc8-511b-be71-d07c04b80a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i + 2j + 4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123072} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdd6503-ab90-5063-bf80-5d64dfc58891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i + j + 4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123073}] :answer-field/id #uuid "52fb938b-ddb8-55c8-9990-18252f0b9f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284eed2b-f5b5-55e8-8043-dc7f9352df8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385391} {:answer/id #uuid "891daeba-0e3b-599d-ba1c-f3ca19efea95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385392} {:answer/id #uuid "7479fa2d-d3ba-5e5f-b91f-c455aeddd593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385393} {:answer/id #uuid "f368beea-18d3-5ba4-a48e-57ffd46d193b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385394} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb18261-78e6-5c3a-b541-e09f14d04051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7479fa2d-d3ba-5e5f-b91f-c455aeddd593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385393} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6793d18-67c7-5552-bd0c-a7ad3c9a1562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5878a311-1e95-57bd-8bd7-391f384f5bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37570" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+5x+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(y+5)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(-1+5)\\,i+(1)\\,j+2(2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,i+j+4\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e84be6-fb21-5888-bde0-2b1fc8861800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403470} {:answer/id #uuid "125b86ba-8a17-5cc7-bb9b-688c88040c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403471} {:answer/id #uuid "a563a551-487d-52b0-b121-65d93d53f35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403472} {:answer/id #uuid "74e61dbd-b0b0-51fb-aee1-a797c08c8fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403473} {:answer/id #uuid "65d0bd41-c0f7-5813-a9d5-53b92a3b0d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65d0bd41-c0f7-5813-a9d5-53b92a3b0d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a86f3ea-971f-5c0c-ab0d-7a86359ca0e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca7dad4d-8007-5c21-9019-38d66b5366c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71407" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4(\\frac{1}{2})-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}]}
